1 GOTO
    The GOTO command goes on at a labeled line of the command procedure.
    A label is a name followed by a colon at the start of a command line,
    alone or before a command:

        $ LOOP:
        $ COUNT = COUNT + 1
        $ IF COUNT .LT. 10 THEN GOTO LOOP

    Syntax:

        GOTO label

    A label is known once the procedure has run past it; GOTO takes the
    one run past last, and otherwise searches forward for the first one.
    A label inside an IF block or a subroutine (see HELP CALL) that the
    GOTO isn't in can't be gone to.  When the label isn't found, GOTO
    reports it (DCL-W-USGOTO), and the procedure ends.  Labels belong to
    their command level: a procedure, or a CALL subroutine.  At the
    terminal a label is ignored (DCL-W-NOLBLS).  GOTO leaves $STATUS as
    it was.
